["<aside class=\"callout why\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>A moose does not think like a deer. It is a calm, solitary giant that stares, walks off, comes to a call and sometimes fights back. Know its habits and its tempers.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four cards on moose social life. Mostly alone, with no herd and no year round pecking order. The only lasting bond is cow and calf, for about one year, until the cow chases it off before her next calf in May. In the rut cows gather near a prime bull and choose their mate. In winter small groups gather at good food but have no real social bond.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A loner with one bond</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"40\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mostly alone</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">no herd, no year round</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">pecking order</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"40\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"295\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cow and calf</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the one bond: 1 year</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">cow chases it off in May</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"160\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">cows gather near a prime</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bull; cows choose</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"160\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"295\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Winter</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">small groups at good food</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">no real social bond</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC moose account, 2000 (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Mostly alone; the cow and calf are the one bond <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>No herd and no year round pecking order <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this chapter covers</summary><ul><li>Alarm and flight, how a moose learns pressure, social life, cow and calf, the rut bull, danger to people, and the habits a hunter can predict.</li><li>Previous: <a href=\"#/s/mc-moose-03\">Chapter 3, The senses</a>. Next: <a href=\"#/s/mc-moose-05\">Chapter 5, The year</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-cow.jpg\" alt=\"Cow moose feeding in a lake, Glacier National Park. No antlers, long pale legs, brown body.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow moose feeding in a lake, Glacier National Park. No antlers, long pale legs, brown body. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: naturenps, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_moose_(33233257795).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Often stares, then trots off into thick cover (Tip).</li><li>Runs up to about 55 km/h; swims strongly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: alarm and flight in detail</summary><ul><li>Escape style (Tip, hunter consensus, <a href=\"#/s/escape-terrain\">escape terrain</a>): a moose often stands and stares, then trots off steadily into the thickest timber or willow, or into water. It rarely bounds like a mule deer.</li><li>Speed: \"Moose can run 35 mph and easily swim 10 miles without stopping\" (75%, Minnesota Department of Natural Resources). That is about 55 km/h and 16 km.</li><li>Alaska reports moose apparently swimming about 10 km (six miles) across Clarence Strait (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li><li>A trotting moose steps over logs and windfall you must climb around (Tip). You will not catch it. Let it settle.</li><li>Moose spotted from an aircraft may not be hunted on that tip (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-unlawful-acts\">unlawful acts</a>).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 298\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five predictable moose habits a hunter can use. 1, it often stands and stares, which can give a shot. 2, in the rut it may walk in to a call. 3, it feeds in the open at first and last light. 4, it comes back to the same willow patch. 5, it crosses roads at dusk and at night.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"298\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">5 predictable moose habits</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"62\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"67\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">1</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Stands and stares</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">often gives a shot</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"110\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">2</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Answers a call</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">in the rut, may walk in</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"158\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">3</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Feeds in the open</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">at first and last light</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"206\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"211\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">4</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"204\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Comes back</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">to the same willow patch</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"254\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"259\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">5</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Crosses roads</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">at dusk and at night</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hunter wisdom and BC sources, labelled in text</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Roads and open cutblocks made moose easier to kill <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Pressured moose stick to cover by day (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: pressure in detail</summary><ul><li>BC moose research <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: the \"landscape change hypothesis\" says beetle salvage logging and new roads made moose more vulnerable \"primarily from hunters and predators\". Survival should rise when cutblocks grow back enough to block the view and logging roads become impassable.</li><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: moose need nearby cover \"to hide from predators and hunters, for shade in hot weather, and for shelter during blizzards\".</li><li>Hunter consensus (Tip): road hunted moose learn to cross roads at dark and feed in openings only at first and last light; a quiet walk in beats a slow drive.</li><li>No moose study on learning and memory of pressure was found: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> detail. Treat the rest as Tip.</li><li>Hunting from a motor vehicle and herding wildlife with a vehicle or boat are unlawful (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-unlawful-acts\">unlawful acts</a>).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four cards on moose social life. Mostly alone, with no herd and no year round pecking order. The only lasting bond is cow and calf, for about one year, until the cow chases it off before her next calf in May. In the rut cows gather near a prime bull and choose their mate. In winter small groups gather at good food but have no real social bond.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A loner with one bond</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"40\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mostly alone</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">no herd, no year round</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">pecking order</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"40\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"295\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cow and calf</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the one bond: 1 year</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">cow chases it off in May</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"160\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">cows gather near a prime</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bull; cows choose</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"160\" width=\"182\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"295\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Winter</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">small groups at good food</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">no real social bond</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC moose account, 2000 (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bg-moose-cow-calf-bowron.jpg\" alt=\"Cow and calf in a wetland, Bowron Lake Park, BC.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow and calf in a wetland, Bowron Lake Park, BC. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Trougnouf, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_in_Bowron_Lake_Provincial_Park,_BC_(DSCF3983).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Seasonal home range: seldom over 5 to 10 km² <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Groups at good food in winter have no social bond <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: social life in detail</summary><ul><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: \"Moose are essentially solitary animals that move about within familiar summer and winter home ranges.\"</li><li>\"Moose do not defend their home range and do not have year round dominance hierarchies like the more social Elk and Bighorn Sheep.\"</li><li>In northern shrublands they form groups of up to 8 to 10 in the rut; less so in forest <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Several moose at a mineral lick or great feeding place form a group \"but these groups lack a social basis\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Unlike other hoofed animals, cows \"may be quite aggressive toward one another\" and receptive cows choose their mate <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-cow-calf.jpg\" alt=\"Cow and calf: if you see a calf, the cow is close.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow and calf: if you see a calf, the cow is close. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiras_Moose_Cow_and_Calf_Seedskadee_NWR_(15072335119).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-calf.jpg\" alt=\"Spring calf: red brown and gangly.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Spring calf: red brown and gangly. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWSAlaska, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_calf_(50007803527).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The bond lasts about one year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>A cow defends her calf hard <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/f/ff/Moose_calf_walking.webm/Moose_calf_walking.webm.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>A calf walking: the cow is never far away. <span class=\"credit\">Video: Alaska Region U.S. Fish &amp; Wildlife Service, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_calf_walking.webm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: cow and calf in detail</summary><ul><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: \"The Moose's only lasting social bond is the bond between mother and calf, and it lasts for only a year.\" Cows protect calves from predators and lead them to the best habitats.</li><li>Pregnant cows seek seclusion in late May and June and chase away last year's young <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. That is why lone yearlings wander in spring (Tip).</li><li>Calves stay with their mothers 12 to 18 months (75%, Minnesota Department of Natural Resources).</li><li>\"A cow moose defends her newborn calf vigorously\" (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li><li>Black bears can take many newborn calves; wolves kill moose all year (90%, BC account). BC collared calves died mostly from predation: 65% wolf, 9% bear, 7% cougar (90%, BC research report 2022).</li><li>Hunting point: in Regions 3 and 8 the calf and the cow are both closed to you without an LEH (Limited Entry Hunting) draw (99%, chapter 10).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 384\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A cow moose, side view, showing three warning signs before a charge: 1, ears laid back; 2, the long hair on the hump raised; 3, licking its lips. If you see this you are far too close: back away and get a tree between you.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"384\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Angry moose: 3 signs</text>\n<rect x=\"8\" y=\"44\" width=\"384\" height=\"270\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e8eef0\"/>\n<path d=\"M8 296 C100 288 300 292 392 284 V306 H8z\" fill=\"#b9c79a\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(218 300) scale(0.98)\"><path d=\"M-62 -108 L-59 -50 L-62 0\" stroke=\"#6e5844\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M92 -108 L95 -50 L92 0\" stroke=\"#6e5844\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M-95 -150 C-90 -185 -60 -205 -30 -200 C10 -192 60 -178 100 -170 C125 -165 136 -150 131 -130 C127 -112 112 -103 92 -103 L-58 -103 C-84 -103 -100 -120 -95 -150z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-76 -110 L-79 -52 L-75 -4\" stroke=\"#9a8774\" stroke-width=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M-82 -3 L-68 -3\" stroke=\"#1b1b1b\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M78 -110 L75 -52 L79 -4\" stroke=\"#9a8774\" stroke-width=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M72 -3 L86 -3\" stroke=\"#1b1b1b\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M-78 -194 L-70 -216 L-66 -199 L-62 -209 L-58 -198 L-54 -215 L-50 -197 L-46 -208 L-42 -197 L-38 -214 L-34 -196 L-30 -207 L-26 -195 L-22 -212 L-18 -194 L-14 -206 L-10 -193 L-6 -211 L-2 -193 L2 -205 L6 -192  L4 -190z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-96 -150 C-104 -160 -108 -172 -104 -184 C-96 -196 -80 -200 -66 -196 L-60 -170z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-98 -184 C-112 -192 -132 -190 -148 -180 C-162 -172 -174 -162 -180 -150 C-185 -140 -180 -130 -170 -131 C-160 -132 -150 -136 -140 -134 C-126 -132 -112 -138 -100 -150z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-180 -150 C-186 -140 -182 -128 -172 -128\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6e5844\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"-176\" cy=\"-146\" rx=\"3\" ry=\"2\" fill=\"#120c08\"/><path d=\"M-126 -134 C-128 -124 -126 -112 -122 -104 C-118 -112 -116 -124 -116 -134z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><circle cx=\"-136\" cy=\"-170\" r=\"3.2\" fill=\"#f4ecd8\"/><circle cx=\"-137\" cy=\"-170\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#120c08\"/><path d=\"M-112 -184 C-100 -190 -86 -196 -70 -200 C-84 -188 -98 -182 -110 -180z\" fill=\"#6e5844\" stroke=\"#120c08\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M-166 -131 C-170 -124 -164 -118 -158 -122 C-156 -126 -160 -130 -162 -131z\" fill=\"#d97f8a\" stroke=\"#a24d5a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g>\n<line x1=\"129.8\" y1=\"111.84\" x2=\"56\" y2=\"83\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"129.8\" cy=\"111.84\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<rect x=\"42.0\" y=\"54.0\" width=\"127.7\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"105.838\" y=\"74.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ears laid back</text>\n<circle cx=\"26.0\" cy=\"68.5\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"26\" y=\"73.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"182.72\" y1=\"96.16\" x2=\"344\" y2=\"83\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"182.72\" cy=\"96.16\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<rect x=\"238.9\" y=\"54.0\" width=\"119.1\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"298.442\" y=\"74.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hump hair up</text>\n<circle cx=\"374.0\" cy=\"68.5\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"374\" y=\"73.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n<line x1=\"58.26\" y1=\"178.48\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"58.26\" cy=\"178.48\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<rect x=\"42.0\" y=\"222.0\" width=\"68.4\" height=\"49.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"76.2208\" y=\"242.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Licks</text>\n<text x=\"76.2208\" y=\"262.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">its lips</text>\n<circle cx=\"26.0\" cy=\"246.5\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"26\" y=\"251.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n<rect x=\"8\" y=\"316\" width=\"384\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fbe4d6\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"341\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">See this? You are far too close.</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"363\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Back away. Get a tree between you.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Cows with calves and rutting bulls can attack <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>In Alaska, moose injure more people than bears each year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: danger in detail</summary><ul><li>Alaska Department of Fish and Game <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>: \"In late spring and summer, cow moose with young calves are very protective and will attack humans who come too close.\" \"During the fall mating season in late September and October, termed the rut, bull moose may be aggressive toward humans.\"</li><li>Hungry, tired moose in deep snow and moose harassed by people, dogs and traffic also turn aggressive <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>\"Moose view dogs as enemies and will sometimes go out of their way to kick at one\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Your dog: leashed for moose anyway (99%, page 12, <a href=\"#/s/rb-legal-methods\">legal methods</a>).</li><li>Warning signs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>: \"The long hairs on its hump are raised, ears laid back (much like a dog or cat), and it may lick its lips.\" A moose walking slowly toward you is not being friendly.</li><li>Minnesota <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>: moose \"are seldom aggressive\", except a cow protecting a calf or a bull in the rut.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation. A cow moose charges at a hunter. The hunter runs and puts a tree between them. The moose stops short at the tree. Text: run, put a tree between you; many charges are bluffs, and moose do not chase far, says Alaska Department of Fish and Game.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"36\" width=\"400\" height=\"264\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" opacity=\".5\"/><g transform=\"translate(200 200) scale(1.2)\"><polygon points=\"0,-60 -16,-14 16,-14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><polygon points=\"0,-44 -20,0 20,0\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"-3\" y=\"0\" width=\"6\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/></g><g><g transform=\"translate(0 0) scale(0.6)\"><rect x=\"-36\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-26\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"26\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"36\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-46 0 Q-48 -26 -20 -26 Q10 -40 34 -34 Q48 -26 44 2 Q20 22 -30 18 Q-48 16 -46 0 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M30 -30 L56 -40 L66 -26 L40 0 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M56 -44 L86 -30 Q92 -22 84 -18 L64 -20 Q54 -26 56 -44 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M66 -22 Q66 -6 60 -4 Q58 -12 62 -22 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"54\" cy=\"-50\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"3.5\" transform=\"rotate(-30 54 -50)\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"-34\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#eeeeee\"/></g><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" values=\"40 190;150 190;150 190\" keyTimes=\"0;0.5;1\" dur=\"6s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g><g><g transform=\"translate(0 0) scale(0.8)\"><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-62\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"-8\" y=\"-53\" width=\"16\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"-7\" y=\"-26\" width=\"6\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"1\" y=\"-26\" width=\"6\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/></g><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" values=\"180 240;230 240;250 220\" keyTimes=\"0;0.4;1\" dur=\"6s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"5.5s\" dur=\"0.5s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"46\" width=\"280\" height=\"52\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"68\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Run. Put a tree between you.</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Most charges stop short (ADF&amp;G)</text></g>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Run, and put a tree between you. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Run: moose do not chase far <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Knocked down? Curl up, cover your head, hold still <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: charges in detail</summary><ul><li>Alaska Department of Fish and Game <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>: \"Many charges are 'bluff' charges\", but take them seriously.</li><li>\"Unlike with bears or even dogs, it is usually a good idea to run from a moose because they won't chase you very far.\" \"You can run around a tree faster than a moose.\"</li><li>If knocked down: \"Curl up in a ball, protect your head with your hands, and hold still.\" Do not get up until the moose has moved off, or it may attack again <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Note: this is the opposite of bear advice. Do not mix them up (Tip, <a href=\"#/s/black-bear\">black bear</a>).</li><li>Calling a bull in the rut brings an angry animal toward you on purpose. Keep a tree close and your partner where you both know he is (Tip, chapter 9).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-young-bull.jpg\" alt=\"Young bull: small antlers, slim neck.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young bull: small antlers, slim neck. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GrandTetonNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_Moose_Portrait_(41410522174).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-bull-side.jpg\" alt=\"Mature bull: deep chest, heavy neck, wide palms.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mature bull: deep chest, heavy neck, wide palms. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jacob W. Frank, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alces_alces_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Older bulls do most of the breeding <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Young bulls wander more and come to calls more freely (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: bulls in detail</summary><ul><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: yearling bulls can breed, \"but older bulls are responsible for most of the successful breeding\".</li><li>Prime bulls of equal size shove with locked antlers; the fights mainly show cows who is best; bulls do not hold a harem <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Serious fights are rare, but bulls get punctures, broken ribs and sometimes die of wounds (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li><li>Body clues (Tip, hunter consensus): an old bull has a deep chest, heavy neck, sagging belly and wide heavy palms; a young bull looks long legged and slim, \"a cow with antlers\".</li><li>Hunter consensus (Tip): young bulls, beaten off by older ones, wander and answer calls more readily, which suits a spike fork season.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 298\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five predictable moose habits a hunter can use. 1, it often stands and stares, which can give a shot. 2, in the rut it may walk in to a call. 3, it feeds in the open at first and last light. 4, it comes back to the same willow patch. 5, it crosses roads at dusk and at night.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"298\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">5 predictable moose habits</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"62\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"67\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">1</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Stands and stares</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">often gives a shot</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"110\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">2</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Answers a call</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">in the rut, may walk in</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"158\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">3</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Feeds in the open</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">at first and last light</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"206\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"211\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">4</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"204\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Comes back</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">to the same willow patch</text><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"254\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"259\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"True\">5</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Crosses roads</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">at dusk and at night</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hunter wisdom and BC sources, labelled in text</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Stares, answers calls, feeds at the edges at first and last light (Tip).</li><li>Comes back to the same willow (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: habits you can plan around</summary><ol><li><strong>The stare</strong> (60%, hunter lore): gives you seconds to count and settle.</li><li><strong>The call</strong> (65%, Outdoor Canada and BC account): in the rut, cows and bulls respond to a cow moan; a bull may walk right in.</li><li><strong>The edges</strong> (75%, BC account and app): feeding where willow, water and cover meet, mostly near dawn and dusk.</li><li><strong>The same patch</strong> (Tip): moose feed on the same browse for days; fresh sign means come back.</li><li><strong>Roads at dusk</strong> (70%, road safety sources): moose cross and lick roads at dusk and at night.</li><li>None of these is guaranteed. A moose in thick cover by day is very hard to find (Tip).</li></ol></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 384\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A cow moose, side view, showing three warning signs before a charge: 1, ears laid back; 2, the long hair on the hump raised; 3, licking its lips. If you see this you are far too close: back away and get a tree between you.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"384\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Angry moose: 3 signs</text>\n<rect x=\"8\" y=\"44\" width=\"384\" height=\"270\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e8eef0\"/>\n<path d=\"M8 296 C100 288 300 292 392 284 V306 H8z\" fill=\"#b9c79a\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(218 300) scale(0.98)\"><path d=\"M-62 -108 L-59 -50 L-62 0\" stroke=\"#6e5844\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M92 -108 L95 -50 L92 0\" stroke=\"#6e5844\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M-95 -150 C-90 -185 -60 -205 -30 -200 C10 -192 60 -178 100 -170 C125 -165 136 -150 131 -130 C127 -112 112 -103 92 -103 L-58 -103 C-84 -103 -100 -120 -95 -150z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-76 -110 L-79 -52 L-75 -4\" stroke=\"#9a8774\" stroke-width=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M-82 -3 L-68 -3\" stroke=\"#1b1b1b\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M78 -110 L75 -52 L79 -4\" stroke=\"#9a8774\" stroke-width=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M72 -3 L86 -3\" stroke=\"#1b1b1b\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M-78 -194 L-70 -216 L-66 -199 L-62 -209 L-58 -198 L-54 -215 L-50 -197 L-46 -208 L-42 -197 L-38 -214 L-34 -196 L-30 -207 L-26 -195 L-22 -212 L-18 -194 L-14 -206 L-10 -193 L-6 -211 L-2 -193 L2 -205 L6 -192  L4 -190z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-96 -150 C-104 -160 -108 -172 -104 -184 C-96 -196 -80 -200 -66 -196 L-60 -170z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-98 -184 C-112 -192 -132 -190 -148 -180 C-162 -172 -174 -162 -180 -150 C-185 -140 -180 -130 -170 -131 C-160 -132 -150 -136 -140 -134 C-126 -132 -112 -138 -100 -150z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><path d=\"M-180 -150 C-186 -140 -182 -128 -172 -128\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6e5844\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"-176\" cy=\"-146\" rx=\"3\" ry=\"2\" fill=\"#120c08\"/><path d=\"M-126 -134 C-128 -124 -126 -112 -122 -104 C-118 -112 -116 -124 -116 -134z\" fill=\"#3b2a1e\"/><circle cx=\"-136\" cy=\"-170\" r=\"3.2\" fill=\"#f4ecd8\"/><circle cx=\"-137\" cy=\"-170\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#120c08\"/><path d=\"M-112 -184 C-100 -190 -86 -196 -70 -200 C-84 -188 -98 -182 -110 -180z\" fill=\"#6e5844\" stroke=\"#120c08\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M-166 -131 C-170 -124 -164 -118 -158 -122 C-156 -126 -160 -130 -162 -131z\" fill=\"#d97f8a\" stroke=\"#a24d5a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g>\n<line x1=\"129.8\" y1=\"111.84\" x2=\"56\" y2=\"83\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"129.8\" cy=\"111.84\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<rect x=\"42.0\" y=\"54.0\" width=\"127.7\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"105.838\" y=\"74.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ears laid back</text>\n<circle cx=\"26.0\" cy=\"68.5\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"26\" y=\"73.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"182.72\" y1=\"96.16\" x2=\"344\" y2=\"83\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"182.72\" cy=\"96.16\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<rect x=\"238.9\" y=\"54.0\" width=\"119.1\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"298.442\" y=\"74.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hump hair up</text>\n<circle cx=\"374.0\" cy=\"68.5\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"374\" y=\"73.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n<line x1=\"58.26\" y1=\"178.48\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"58.26\" cy=\"178.48\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<rect x=\"42.0\" y=\"222.0\" width=\"68.4\" height=\"49.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"76.2208\" y=\"242.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Licks</text>\n<text x=\"76.2208\" y=\"262.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">its lips</text>\n<circle cx=\"26.0\" cy=\"246.5\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"26\" y=\"251.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n<rect x=\"8\" y=\"316\" width=\"384\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fbe4d6\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"341\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">See this? You are far too close.</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"363\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Back away. Get a tree between you.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Ears back, hair high, lips licked: get a tree, be quick.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 1 bond, 1 year: cow and calf <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (tradition): \"Where there's a calf, there's a cow. Where there's a cow in October, there may be a bull.\"</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Bear: stand tall or play dead. Moose: run round a tree instead.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 55 and 16: runs about 55 km/h, swims 16 km <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 5 to 10: seasonal home range in km² <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (widely reported, opinion): \"Never get between a cow and her calf.\"</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (opinion): \"A moose walking slowly toward you is not your friend.\"</li></ul><aside class=\"callout mistake\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Walking closer to a cow and calf \"for a photo\".</li><li>Letting a dog off leash near moose.</li><li>Treating a moose charge like a bear charge.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>On your next scouting walk, pick a \"charge tree\" each time you stop near willow or water. Make it a habit.</p></aside></details>","<ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, Lands and Parks (2000), <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Moose in British Columbia\"</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></li><li>Alaska Department of Fish and Game, <a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=livewith.aggressivemoose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aggressive moose</a> and <a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/static/education/wns/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moose notebook</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Minnesota Department of Natural Resources, <a href=\"https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/mammals/moose.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moose</a>, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></li><li>BC Ministry of Forests (2023), <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/environment/plants-animals-and-ecosystems/wildlife-wildlife-habitat/moose/2022_moose_research_progress_report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moose research progress report, May 2022</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></li><li>Ken Bailey, Outdoor Canada (2012), <a href=\"https://www.outdoorcanada.ca/?p=3913\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Make like a cow moose\"</a>, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, pages 11 and 12, via <a href=\"#/s/rb-unlawful-acts\">unlawful acts</a> and <a href=\"#/s/rb-legal-methods\">legal methods</a> <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li><a href=\"#/sources\">Hunt Mentor research notes (summary of the sources listed in Sources)</a>.</li></ul>"]